  "thought": "Starting a fresh independent read-only re-review of finding F1 from PLAN2.P3.4 round1.\n\nReview scope is strictly F1. Only four product files changed. I will read the P3.4 evidence and confirm the fix without regressions. I will return GREEN or exact FINDINGS in Russian.\n\nI will not spawn a subagent or use MCP tools. I will perform the review entirely with native Read, Search, and ListDir.Reading manifests, changed source files, dependency references, tests, and evidence receipts.Shell and Python execution are unavailable. I will rely on native Read, Search, and ListDir tools for this review.Carefully verifying the dependency closure for F1.\n\nChecking RecordActivityCommand column mapping, ActivityStoreRepository persistence, RecordsActivity savepoint behavior, SchemaBuilder UUID declaration, and RequestContext requestId handling.\n\nFour kernel event mappings are under review: grant created/revoked and share created/revoked. The correlation_id preservation logic is being checked against the norm requiring it in activity.context.\n\nConfirmed that null correlation_id is correctly omitted, and the spread order preserves both correlation_id and system_reason without overwrites. Now verifying that RequestContext::requestId returns the request UUID rather than the correlation identifier.\n\nThe correction uses requestId=$request->requestId. This is the prescribed fix. The existing invalid-client-header policy is out of scope. A non-UUID client header could still trigger the same 22P02 error.\n\nThe test fixture supplies a valid UUID header. The correction is limited to requestId=$request->requestId per the round1 finding. The reviewer is checking whether RequestContext passes arbitrary client headers through without validation.\n\nAll four kernel event mappings route through the same requestId assignment. recordForNode correctly adds correlation_id to context when provided, and leaves existing behavior unchanged for Drive callers that omit it.\n\nThe delta only changes ActivityRecorder. Previously record() placed correlationId into request_id. Now it always uses requestId=$request->requestId. Drive callers that previously relied on this fallback behavior will see a null request_id when no correlationId is provided.\n\nThe old code explicitly overwrote request_id with correlationId. This is a behavior change for callers that previously relied on this fallback.\n\nThe old source patch is needed to confirm the exact prior assignment. If the previous code used `requestId: $correlationId ?? $request->requestId`, then a null correlationId preserved the request UUID. The new code always uses `$request->requestId`, which is the intended fix. This would be a regression for callers that previously relied on the null fallback.\n\nThe null-correlation case is resolved. Previously a null correlationId likely left request_id empty. The correction now restores the request UUID there, matching the norm and fixing the bug rather than regressing it.\n\nChecking whether context column JSON size or reserved keys could conflict with correlation_id persistence. Token leakage is confirmed safe because share-created context only stores token_prefix, not the full token. ActorRef system type and id fields still need verification for correct system_reason separation. An empty string correlationId would be written to context.correlation_id, since the guard only checks for null.\n\nEmpty string correlationId is unlikely because OperationContext normally supplies a ULID. It would not trigger the 22P02 error, so this is a minor edge case rather than a new defect.\n\nThe four kernel event mappings do not inject their own correlation_id into the context array. The parameter therefore cannot overwrite caller-supplied context keys.\n\nThe context array spread order is verified safe. When correlationId is present, system_reason is appended after it without collision. If actor reason exists but correlationId is null, the context array is initialized as empty before system_reason is added.\n\nThe context array is mutated before the command is built, so system_reason correctly inherits the updated correlation_id. The reviewer is now checking whether requestId is cast or validated as UUID before insertion to prevent PostgreSQL 22P02 errors.\n\nThe four-mutation test fixture supplies a valid UUID header, so the PostgreSQL green path is covered. The reviewer will verify that RequestContext::requestId is strictly the request UUID field and not the correlation identifier. Activity feature flag and savepoint behavior are noted as out of scope.\n\nRecordsActivity is confirmed unchanged and out of scope. The four event classes still expose correlationId as a ULID, so the mappings must pass $event->correlationId rather than an arbitrary value. OperationContext::system() generates the ULID.\n\nThe reviewer is now checking whether any event subscribers invoke record() through paths outside the four kernel mappings. If a different call site still assigns correlationId to request_id, that would be an incomplete fix within the closure.\n\nULID producers in controllers, filament, and OperationContext remain unchanged. They still pass the ULID as correlationId on events. The fix is isolated to the recorder sink. The reviewer will check ActivityStoreRepository and other RecordActivityCommand callers only if they fall within the changed dependency closure.\n\nChecking whether ActivityRecorder is the sole sink setting requestId on RecordActivityCommand. If so, the fix is complete for this dependency closure.\n\nThe reviewer will also inspect review-diff.patch to confirm the UPGRADE and CHANGELOG wording changes are strictly limited to storage-location references and do not introduce false claims.\n\nThe four-mutation test asserts only a count of four and property presence, not verb identity per row. A silent double-fire or swallowed event could still yield four rows with incorrect verbs. The reviewer is checking whether the test actually validates each verb.Beginning the focused review of F1 by reading the relevant files to check for defects.\n\nChecking four specific areas for the F1 delta: JSON encoding and correlation_id filtering in ActivityStoreRepository::record(), unchanged swallowing behavior in RecordsActivity::safely(), JSON context column type and length limits in SchemaBuilder, and whether the requestIdOf fix actually prevents 22P02.\n\nThe requestIdOf fix does not prevent 22P02 if the request header contains a non-UUID value. Round1 explicitly excluded this existing policy from the newly introduced finding. No new defect is present.\n\nConfirmed requestId is the request UUID path, not correlation. OperationContext keeps correlationId separate. The context column JSON type accepts ULID values. Array spread order is safe because the explicit key overwrites any caller value. Empty string correlationId is possible but unlikely.\n\nNull correlationId leaves the context untouched and only requestId is written. When correlationId is provided, it is appended to context without replacing requestId. ActorRef handling also preserves both correlation_id and system_reason in the JSON context.\n\nChecking whether the review diff is the full round1 workset or only the correction delta. Also verifying if the Activity model casts context to an array and whether a dedicated correlation_id column is missing.\n\nConfirmed correlation_id lives in the JSON context, not a separate column. The SchemaBuilder comment about request_id being an opaque correlation id is pre-existing and unrelated to this delta. The UUID column type is what matters.\n\nConfirmed the four event mappings that invoke record() all pass correlationId as a separate parameter, never requestId. Also checking whether context JSON could exceed column limits or lose keys through casting.\n\nConfirmed the optional correlationId parameter was already present in round1. The correction only changes where that value is stored, not the public API. No breaking change was introduced.\n\nThe manifest hash changed because ActivityRecorder was modified, but its public signature likely remained identical to round1. The documentation wording in both UPGRADE and CHANGELOG accurately reflects the code: correlation_id is stored in activity.context while request_id retains the UUID.I need to examine RequestContext more carefully in relation to F1.\n\nThe invalid-client-header policy is pre-existing and outside F1. The correction only stops injecting OperationContext correlation ULIDs into request_id. Tests with valid UUID headers pass.\n\nNo new defects found. Missing headers and off-request calls remain safe. PostgreSQL jsonb accepts the ULID string. A potential context key collision exists because four mappers omit correlation_id while record() injects it.\n\nThe explicit correlationId parameter correctly overrides any correlation_id already present in the caller's context array. The system_reason merge logic is also being reviewed.\n\nThe system_reason merge is safe. It appends without overwriting correlation_id.\n\nActorRef logic remains unchanged. The recorder still reads actor->type and actor->id from the provided actor object. The delta only modified correlation placement and requestId source.\n\nEmpty correlationId strings are harmless. They pass through jsonb and never reach the UUID column or request_id.\n\nThe next step is verifying that event classes carry OperationContext correlation values rather than request UUIDs.\n\nEvent classes were unchanged. They still carry OperationContext ULIDs. The recorder now places that ULID in context.correlation_id and the request UUID in request_id. This confirms the separation is correct.\n\nVerifying event property names and OperationContext::system. Actor::system is also being checked.\n\nThe four-mutation test covers grant, create, revoke grant, and revoke share. Activity feature defaults to enabled in tests. The green PostgreSQL receipt confirms the test passed with four rows. I will read the receipt to confirm it actually ran those assertions.\n\nThe test only asserts four rows exist, not that all four verbs were recorded. A single operation might emit multiple activity rows, or one operation might fail to write while the total still reaches four. I need to trace event dispatch to confirm each verb actually produced a row.\n\nToken leakage is also being checked. The share context stores token_prefix rather than token, and the test verifies that minted tokens are absent from activity attributes.\n\nI need to read the ActivityRecorder patch to confirm exactly what changed. The recordGrantRevoked method still passes tenant from the node, which is pre-existing behavior. I will not flag this unless the delta modified it.The round1 bug was a missing `$request->` prefix on `requestId`.\n\nThe current code restores `requestId: $request->requestId` and adds `correlation_id` to the context when available. When `correlationId` is null, the context remains unchanged and the request UUID is used. This matches pre-P3.4 behavior.\n\nThe merge order is correct. `correlation_id` is injected into the context before the actor reason is appended, so both values coexist without conflict. If the caller already supplied a `correlation_id`, the parameter value overwrites it.\n\nThe new parameter defaults to null, so existing drive callers are unaffected. The only risk is a caller who explicitly passes a correlationId and also supplies a context array containing the same key; that would overwrite their value.\n\nThe signature change was introduced in P3.4, not this correction. The ULID string is valid JSON and will not trigger a 22P02 error. The context array cast must be verified to ensure the test accessing `$row->context['correlation_id']` succeeds.\n\nAll four kernel mappings forward the correlationId parameter correctly. The token share context and system actor type/id mapping remain unchanged from the original implementation, so neither is a regression.\n\nThe invalid-requestId header path is out of scope for this review. The correction does not introduce it, and the user explicitly excluded it from F1. I will not raise it as a finding.\n\nThe system-actor test path confirms `correlation_id` survives the context merge when `system_reason` is appended, because the actor reason is non-null. This rules out a regression in that specific code path.\n\nEmpty actor reason is a pre-existing behavior and outside this delta. I will grep the patch for UPGRADE or CHANGELOG to verify documentation claims are limited to storage-location wording. I also need to read the evidence receipts to confirm the reported test results.\n\nI will read the evidence JSON files to verify the reported test results rather than trusting the prompt blindly. I also need to confirm the Activity model casts context as an array or jsonb, because forceFill with an array could fail or double-encode if the cast is missing.\n\nAdding a string key to existing context arrays is safe. The four-mutation test sets the request header before operations, so UUID generation works correctly. OperationContext::system ULID values never reach request_id; controllers pass them as correlationId, not as the request header.\n\nGrantController and Filament still pass the ULID as correlationId, not request_id. The record() signature does not include a correlationId parameter, so drive callers cannot supply one.\n\nThe signature change was introduced in P3.4, not this correction. The parameter is optional and defaults to null, so drive callers remain unaffected. I am now checking whether the injected `correlation_id` could overwrite a meaningful key already set by the four kernel mappings.\n\nThe four kernel mappings never set a `correlation_id` key, so injecting it cannot clobber existing context values. Array spread with string keys is also safe.\n\nOperationContext rejects whitespace-only correlation strings. Old events without a valid correlationId are skipped, leaving request_id as the UUID. The four kernel actions always pass a non-empty string from their constructor validation, so all four paths write the value to context.\n\nThe four-mutation test asserts four rows with the request UUID and a ULID correlation, but it does not verify that the four verbs are distinct. A single mutation could write zero events and another two, still yielding four total rows.\n\nEach kernel action emits exactly one activity event. The four-mutation test asserts four rows, which proves no extra listeners wrote activity. RecordsActivity is the sole listener for these events, so the count is exact.\n\nThe PostgreSQL evidence receipt shows exactly four rows. A single mapping still writing the ULID to request_id would have produced only three rows, because the savepoint would swallow that failed insert. This confirms all four kernel mappings persisted correctly.\n\nThe red receipt must be read to confirm it shows the old missing-row failure mode, while the green receipt confirms the fix. The regression suite shows 117 V1-r2 tests passed, 27 V2-r2 passed, 20 correlation-regression tests passed, and both PHPStan and Pint are green. I will inspect the evidence JSON files to verify the row counts and ensure no failures were fabricated.\n\nManifest comparison confirms only four files differ between the two JSONs: CHANGELOG.md, UPGRADE.md, ActivityRecorder.php, and CoreOperationsSecurityTest.php. The base_commit key is identical, so the file set is unchanged. The current ActivityRecorder source matches the described fix. Filesystem digests were not recomputed because shell access is forbidden.\n\nI checked whether an empty string correlationId could slip through the null guard. OperationContext rejects empty values, so this path is already blocked. It would only store an empty string in context, which is not a new defect.\n\nThe optional `?string` type is safe. The parameter is the normative source, so overwriting a caller-supplied context key is correct. If the Activity model casts context as an array, Eloquent JSON-encodes the ULID; a PostgreSQL jsonb column with a check constraint is unlikely.Checking ActorRef for system type and id null, and that reason is preserved.\n\nConfirmed the merge order preserves correlation_id before system_reason in the context array.\n\nThe explicit correlationId parameter correctly overwrites any existing correlation_id in the caller's context. ActorRef system handling remains unchanged from round 1, so no regression is expected.The green log is a summary JSON, not the detailed assertion dump.\n\nVerifying the 28 assertions in correlation-PG-green.json against the two tests. The first test checks ActorRef::SYSTEM_TYPE and null actor_id.\n\nThe first test contains 7 assertions. Pest counts each ->toBe and ->not->toContain as a separate assertion. The second test begins with toHaveCount(4) and iterates over events.\n\nThe second test contributes 21 assertions. The first test contributes 7. The total is exactly 28. No skips were recorded.\n\nThe red log shows the old failure: ModelNotFound from sole() finding no Activity row. This matches silent loss. The red command filtered only the first test, so its input fingerprint differs from green.\n\nThe red failure came from the old candidate. The request_id was set to 'system-correlation', which is not a UUID, so the insert failed and the savepoint swallowed the exception. If the test had already been updated to expect a UUID request_id, the failure would have been an assertion mismatch rather than \"No query results\". The error at Builder.php:827 is sole() throwing because no Activity row was found.\n\nThe red test already expected the new sole() assertion. On PostgreSQL, the old candidate still lacks the activity row, so sole() fails even with the old assertion. The red filter covers only the first test.\n\nChecking V1-r2, V2-r2, V3-r2, correlation-regression, phpstan and pint receipts for missed defects. A potential issue: context correlation_id might overwrite an existing caller key if the parameter is non-null. The four mappings do not set this field.\n\nThe correlation_id parameter defaults to null, so drive callers remain unaffected. Both correlationId and actor reason keys may coexist when present. JSONB and integer key collisions were ruled out. The non-UUID X-Request-Id handling in RequestContext is outside the scope of this finding. The original code assigned requestId directly; P3.4 changed it to correlationId ?? requestId.\n\nRestoring the original requestId assignment would simply revert to a pre-existing policy. That is out of scope and not a new defect.\n\nAn empty correlationId is acceptable because OperationContext rejects empty values, and null simply means the key is absent.\n\nLarge context arrays from shares are not a new issue.\n\nrecordForNode passes correlationId through correctly, including in Grant revoked.\n\nAll four record() callers pass $event->correlationId correctly. recordGrantRevoked inherits tenant from its node via recordForNode, which is pre-existing and out of scope. The recorder line structure remains unchanged. I am now checking ActorRef to confirm the system actor id is null, the type is vaulter:system, and the reason flows through properly; a mismatch would break the green test.\n\nThe 28 assertions include system_reason, confirming the test passes. I am now checking whether the Activity model hides context or casts request_id, and verifying that getAttributes() exposes the JSON context so the token check works correctly. The share context uses token_prefix rather than token, and the test asserts the minted token is absent from activity attributes.\n\nThe correlation_id is a ULID from OperationContext, so it cannot accidentally match the minted token. Both documentation files contain the same sentence, which aligns with the user's instruction to limit the change to that explicit storage-location wording. The review-diff.patch is the round1 candidate versus base, not the correction delta, so the old changelog text is not present in this patch.\n\nThe documentation wording is accurate. ActorRef maps correctly, correlation_id appears in context when provided, and request_id retains the UUID from RequestContext.\n\nThe documentation wording is intentionally imprecise about non-UUID request headers, but this is out of scope. The claim that events retain an explicit system reason is accurate.\n\nThe manifest confirms only four files changed. CHANGELOG and UPGRADE differ from their reviewed versions; OperationGuard and GrantNodeAccessAction are unchanged.\n\nActivityRecorder is the only remaining file with a hash mismatch. RevokeNodeAccessAction, CreateShareLinkAction, RevokeShareLinkAction, GrantInput, GrantNodeAccessInput, RevokeGrantInput, CreateShareLinkInput, RevokeShareInput, ShareLinkInput, NodeGrantCreated, NodeGrantRevoked, NodeShareCreated, NodeShareRevoked, GrantController, ActivityController, PublicShareController, ShareController, StoreNodeGrantRequest, StoreShareLinkRequest, AccessOperations and ActivityOperations are all unchanged.\n\nCoreOperationsSecurityTest is the only remaining file with a hash mismatch. All other reviewed files match their reviewed versions.\n\nOperationsBoundaryTest, ActivityResourceTest, NodeGrantResourceTest, NodeShareResourceTest and DriveBuilder are all unchanged. The base commit hash is confirmed as f5987542. I am now verifying that the V1, V2, V3, regression, PHPStan and Pint receipts are green and post-fix timestamps. I will also confirm that the Pint and core PHPStan r2 receipts cover the changed files.\n\nThe four-mutation test requires all four rows to exist and carry the same correlation ULID. A missing correlationId in any mapping would cause a null array access or assertion failure, but every caller passes it correctly. The grant-revoke path uses recordForNode, which is out of scope for this finding; the test does not check tenant, so no regression exists there.\n\nrecordGrantRevoked passes arguments by name. Public record() places correlation_id at the top level of the context array, overriding any existing key. The schema is jsonb nullable, and Eloquent encodes the array correctly for PostgreSQL.\n\nThe test request header is a valid UUID v4, so PostgreSQL accepts it. ULIDs from OperationContext are 26-character Crockford strings and do not violate the jsonb column.\n\nPostgreSQL accepted the ULID in JSON context, confirming the insert succeeded. RecordsActivity was not modified and is out of scope. The test does not assert verb names, but it validates all normative fields for every returned row. A weak test is not a product defect when the code path is correct.\n\nThe unchanged operation methods call each action exactly once. The test passed, confirming the activity subscriber is active and exercised all four mappings.\n\nThe correlation ULID is a plain string, so JSON encoding is not a concern. HTTP tests still pass the ULID as correlationId; after the fix it lands in context while request_id is handled by RequestContext. The HTTP regression test passed, but it may run against SQLite rather than PostgreSQL, which would explain why the 22P02 error is not caught there.\n\nThe PostgreSQL test exercises the same record() path as HTTP controllers. Both pass the ULID correlation through OperationContext, so the sink is covered. The defect was in the storage layer, not the controller. HTTP controllers remain unchanged and still pass the correlationId correctly. This confirms the fix is fully validated.\n\nRequestContext reads X-Correlation-Id as a fallback for X-Request-Id. Controllers do not appear to set that header to the operation ULID, so the ULID cannot leak into request_id through the HTTP layer.\n\nLaravel's Str::uuid() returns a valid PostgreSQL UUID string. Off-request calls leave requestId null, which is safe because the column is nullable and correlation_id remains in context. I am now checking ActorRef and the receipt files.Carefully verifying additional details to confirm a potential defect.\n\nCounting assertions in the green tests. The first test contains 7 assertions. The second test adds one assertion for event count plus four assertions for request IDs.\n\nThe second test adds 21 assertions, bringing the total to 28. The green receipt is consistent with the test source. The red receipt shows sole() found no Activity model. This is the silent loss. The filter only checked the first test. That test wrote a non-UUID correlation ID into request_id.\n\nThe red receipt shows exit 2 with zero assertions and a ModelNotFound error. This confirms the missing row rather than an assertion failure. The 22P02 SQLSTATE is absent from the log but unnecessary to require, since the outcome matches F1's description.\n\nRe-examining the context merge order in the correction. The correlation_id is inserted into $context before system_reason is appended when an actor reason exists. Both keys survive correctly.\n\nThe explicit correlationId parameter correctly overrides any existing correlation_id in the context array. When correlationId is null, the existing context value is preserved. Kernel events may pass null, but the four mutations always supply a non-empty correlationId because OperationContext validates it.\n\nEvents may be constructed without a correlationId, leaving request_id as the request UUID and omitting correlation_id from context. This is acceptable per the norm.\n\nrecordForNode for grant revoked correctly passes correlationId.\n\nThe visibility layer stripping the correlation_id key is out of scope unless the delta breaks the read path. The key is plain JSON in the context field.\n\nThe public API snapshot hash remains unchanged.\n\nThe record() signature already included correlationId in round1, so the correction introduces no breaking change. Documentation wording matches the code exactly across both UPGRADE and CHANGELOG. No other correlation-related claims were altered.Comparing old and new changelog wording to confirm only storage-location wording changed.\n\nReading patch context around lines 15-70 to verify the old sentence and confirm only that wording changed.\n\nChecking that each operation method emits exactly one event. The correlation_id spread order is correct. Now examining the system_reason versus correlation_id ordering.\n\nConfirmed that system_reason and correlation_id coexist in the context without overwriting each other. Empty string correlationId is not a new defect relative to F1.\n\nOperationContext rejects empty strings, so the recorder needs no extra validation. The requestId fallback to Str::uuid() is correct and excludes invalid headers. ULIDs from producers are not copied into the request header.\n\nThe `recordForNode` public seam preserves the original requestId behavior when no correlationId is passed. The parameter was added in round1 with a null default, so the correction does not alter the signature or change existing callers. The previous buggy `??` change is being traced to confirm the restored requestId path.\n\nConfirmed the round1 patch introduced a `??` operator that broke the original request-UUID-only behavior. The correction restores the pre-bug path where `requestId` is always the request UUID, and adds the correlation key to context only when a correlationId is provided. This eliminates the regression for callers that omit the parameter. The four kernel event mappings correctly pass the correlationId.\n\nAll four kernel event paths route through the same lines 177-192. No path still assigns correlation to requestId.\n\nThe share-created context retains its existing keys: token_prefix, permissions, and no plaintext token. Adding correlation_id does not alter this.\n\nActorRef serialization remains unchanged. It still uses the type-id array with system_reason drawn from the actor reason. Tests cover this behavior.\n\nThe grant-revoked path using `recordForNode` is pre-existing and out of scope for this delta. The activity feature and savepoint behavior are unchanged. PostgreSQL green confirms that inserts with UUID request_id and ULID in JSON context succeed, while the red test proves the old code lost the row.\n\nThe red test failure occurs at `sole()` before any assertions run. This confirms the row was missing because the insert failed and was swallowed, not because of a test expectation change.\n\nThe red test tree is confirmed distinct from the green candidate. The zero assertions and ModelNotFound error match the silent savepoint swallow, where the SQLSTATE 22P02 is logged as a warning before sole() fails.\n\nThe green test run reports two tests and twenty-eight assertions. It does not print row contents.\n\nCounting assertions in the green test code. The first test contains seven assertions. The second test iterates over four events and checks request_id, correlation_id, and actor_type for each one.\n\nThe second test adds five assertions per event, totaling twenty-one. Combined with the first test's seven, the green suite reports exactly twenty-eight assertions. The receipt omits literal \"ULID\" or \"four rows\" wording, but the filter includes both tests and the correlation_id assertion checks equality against whatever system() generated.Checking whether the docs change is limited to storage location wording compared to round1.\n\nConfirmed the docs change is strictly the storage-location wording shift. Now verifying the CHANGELOG and UPGRADE sections are otherwise identical.The user confirmed both PHPStan and changed-file Pint checks are green.Checking for a subtle bug that may have been missed.\n\nRe-examined the context merge logic. When both correlationId and actor reason are present, both keys are correctly included in the final context array.\n\nVerified the null actor and user actor paths. A potential bug was noted: correlation_id could overwrite an existing context key of the same name, though none of the four kernel mappers currently include that key.\n\nConfirmed the spread order correctly prioritizes the correlationId parameter over any existing correlation_id in the caller context. Empty string correlationIds are rejected by OperationContext and would not cause a PostgreSQL error or regress F1.\n\nRequestContext non-UUID values are explicitly out of scope. The fix correctly uses $request->requestId, falling back to Str::uuid() when no header is present. This pre-existing behavior is not part of the current finding.\n\nThe four-mutation test does not prove ULID versus UUID in context. OperationContext::system() generates a ULID by default, but the test asserts that request_id and correlationId are different values. PostgreSQL green passed both assertions, confirming neither value was stored as the other.\n\nCounting Pest assertions precisely. The first test contains seven assertions. The second test adds one assertion for event count and four more for request_id values.\n\nThe second test contributes 21 assertions, bringing the total to 28. The receipt matches exactly.\n\nThe red run was the pre-fix tree. It used a different fingerprint and exited with ModelNotFound because sole() found no row. This confirms the insert was swallowed before the query.\n\nThe red receipt does not literally contain the PostgreSQL error code 22P02. Round1 already established the mechanism, and Round2 confirms the symptom on the old candidate while the fix shows no such symptom. The Eloquent Builder sole() failure is a query result issue, not a database connection error.\n\nThe query ran and found zero rows. That is the F1 symptom.\n\nRecordsActivity.php was not in the manifest delta. The current source still swallows inserts. The fix does not depend on changing that file. After the fix, the insert uses a valid UUID so the savepoint no longer rolls it back. PostgreSQL green proves the row exists.\n\nFour mappings were traced. Grant created uses record() with event correlationId and verb GrantCreated. Grant revoked u